  "logCaption": {
   "cap": "The landscape of $H_t$ over the $z$-plane: the height is $\\operatorname{Im} H_t$ and the colour $\\arg H_t$ (bar); $H_t$ is drawn on 5 surfaces, one for each of the sheets $k=-2,\\dots,2$ of $F$. Each deformed logarithm runs along $(z^{u}e^{2\\pi iku}-1)/u+u$, $u=1-t$, where $k$ is its branch number on that surface. Shown at $t=1.00$, where $H_t=f_{2}\\circ G_t$ deforms $f_{2}$ ($t=0$) into $F(z)=\\sin\\left(\\log z\\right)$ ($t=1$); it is the homotopy $H_t=f_{2}\\circ G_t$, $0\\le t\\le 1$, where $G_t$ is the homotopy $G_t(z)=(z^{1-t}-1)/(1-t)+1-t$ for $0\\le t<1$, $G_1(z)=\\log z$ (on every sheet). Here $F=f_{2}\\circ f_{1}$ with $f_{1}(z)=\\log z$ and $f_{2}(w)=\\sin w$. On its natural domain $D=\\{z : \\arg z\\neq 180^\\circ\\}$, $F$ is analytic; this is guaranteed by the chain.",
   "title": "<span class=\"ptitle\"><i>z</i>-plane</span><span class=\"prole\"><span class=\"rf\">landscape of Im <i>H</i><sub><i>t</i></sub>, on the sheets <i>k</i> = −2…2 of <i>F</i></span><span class=\"rs\">landscape</span></span>"
  },
  "pair": {
   "drawn": null,
   "title": "<span class=\"ptitle\"><i>z</i>-plane</span><span class=\"prole\"><span class=\"rf\">landscape of Im <i>H</i><sub><i>t</i></sub></span><span class=\"rs\">landscape</span></span>",
   "tip": "While this deformation shows, one sheet is drawn: a step it deforms has no path on every sheet."
  },
  "caption": "The landscape of $H_t$ over the $z$-plane: the height is $\\operatorname{Im} H_t$ and the colour $\\arg H_t$ (bar); $H_t$ is drawn on 2 surfaces, one for each sheet of $F$. Each deformed root or power $z^{\\alpha}$ runs along $z^{\\sigma}e^{2\\pi ik\\sigma}$, $\\sigma=1+(\\alpha-1)t$, where $k$ is its branch number on that surface. Shown at $t=0.50$, where $H_t=f_{2}\\circ G_t$ deforms $f_{2}$ ($t=0$) into $F(z)=\\sin\\left(\\sqrt{z}\\right)$ ($t=1$); it is the homotopy $H_t=f_{2}\\circ G_t$, $0\\le t\\le 1$, where $G_t$ is the homotopy $G_t(z)=z^{1-(1/2)t}$ (on every sheet). Here $F=f_{2}\\circ f_{1}$ with $f_{1}(z)=\\sqrt{z}$ and $f_{2}(w)=\\sin w$. On its natural domain $D=\\{z : \\arg z\\neq 180^\\circ\\}$, $F$ is analytic; this is guaranteed by the chain.",
  "captionStatic": "The landscape of $F(z)=\\sin\\left(\\sqrt{z}\\right)$ over the $z$-plane: the height is $\\operatorname{Im} F$ and the colour $\\arg F$ (bar); both sheets are shown. Here $F=f_{2}\\circ f_{1}$ with $f_{1}(z)=\\sqrt{z}$ and $f_{2}(w)=\\sin w$. On its natural domain $D=\\{z : \\arg z\\neq 180^\\circ\\}$, $F$ is analytic; this is guaranteed by the chain.",
